              "id": "8767f280-8dea-46be-8599-21fd5e3fe22a",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "<strong>Bernard Herbert Suits</strong> (25 November 1925–05 February 2007), philosopher and professor, was born in Detroit, Michigan. Suits attended Denby High School in Detroit. Suits enlisted in the US Navy and served from 1944 to 1946 as Seaman First Class on the U.S.S. Olmsted. He went on to the University of Chicago where he received his BA and his MA in Philosophy. Suits received his PhD in Philosophy at the University of Illinois.<br><br>While teaching at the University of Waterloo, Suits held the following positions: Chair of the Waterloo Philosophy Department, Associate Dean for Graduate Affairs in the Faculty of Arts and President of the International Association for the Philosophy of Sport. Suits was awarded a Distinguished Teaching Award in 1982 and was appointed as Distinguished Professor Emeritus in 1995.<br>"
            },
            {
              "id": "42e163b6-84ea-4e4c-afd2-1ac102081eef",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "Suits’ area of philosophic inquiry was games and gaming and he became an authority in the field. He is best known for his book “The Grasshopper: Games, Life and Utopia“. Suits was also a visiting professor at the University of Lethbridge and the University of Bristol. In 1982, Suits was a special guest star on the TVO special ”The Academy on Moral Philosophy”."
            },
            {
              "id": "92112793-2bc4-4311-bc6a-2c516c10b791",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
